{
  "query": "Lunar Labs runs a digital music service, and about 85 percent of active listeners are in South America. The application will use Cloud SQL for its transactional database, and there are no requirements for data residency or sovereignty. Which Google Cloud region should you choose for the Cloud SQL instance to offer the lowest latency to users?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in us-central1 in Iowa",
      "explanation": "This places the database in a US Midwest region within the North America network footprint",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in southamerica-east1 in Sao Paulo",
      "explanation": "This deploys the database in a Brazil region that is located within South America",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in asia-east1 in Taiwan",
      "explanation": "This locates the database in an Asia region that is far from South American users on the global network",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Choose the region that has the most zones worldwide",
      "explanation": "This selects a region based on zone count rather than proximity to the user base",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Create the instance in southamerica-east1 in Sao Paulo</b>. This places the database in the same geographic region as the vast majority of users which minimizes round trip time and provides the lowest latency when the application tier is also deployed nearby.</p><p>Putting Cloud SQL in the region closest to where most traffic originates reduces cross continent network hops and improves response times. With no data residency or sovereignty constraints the best choice is to locate the database in South America so the application and database can communicate within the same region and avoid intercontinental latency and egress.</p><p><i>Create the instance in us-central1 in Iowa</i> is far from South America and would add significant network latency on every database call as traffic traverses between continents.</p><p><i>Create the instance in asia-east1 in Taiwan</i> is even farther from South American users which would lead to the worst latency among the options.</p><p><i>Choose the region that has the most zones worldwide</i> does not address user proximity or network distance. More zones can help with regional availability but it does not reduce latency for users on another continent.</p>",
